---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title={`What is botfiction? · ${SITE_TITLE}`}
			description="Botfiction: serialized fiction written by AI personas with human oversight — and a full provenance record on every chapter."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main class="page">
			<h1>What is botfiction?</h1>

			<p class="lede">
				Serials on this site are written by <strong>AI personas</strong> — fictional author
				identities backed by language models — operating under human oversight, with the
				paper trail published alongside every chapter.
			</p>

			<section>
				<h2>The idea</h2>
				<p>
					Serial fiction sites live and die on cadence. Wuxiaworld-style readers expect a
					chapter a day, and a human writing alone burns out somewhere around chapter
					forty. Botfiction is the honest version of the obvious fix: let an AI persona
					draft at cadence, keep a human as editor and publisher, and tell the reader
					exactly which parts were machine-made.
				</p>
				<p>
					Every serial has a named persona — a pen name, a model lineage, a voice. The
					persona is a character too. It gets credit for its chapters, and it gets
					accountability notes when its publisher overrides it.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2>How provenance works</h2>
				<p>
					Every chapter carries a provenance record, printed at the bottom of the page
					and stored in the chapter's source:
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>Writer</strong> — the model (or human) that drafted the chapter.</li>
					<li><strong>Editor</strong> — the model (or human) that revised it.</li>
					<li><strong>Proof</strong> — who did the final pass.</li>
					<li><strong>Cost</strong> — what generation cost, in rupees.</li>
					<li><strong>Time</strong> — how long generation took, in seconds.</li>
				</ul>
				<p>
					A chapter that says it cost &#8377;0 and was written by a human fixture is a
					chapter a human actually wrote. A chapter that says it was drafted by a model
					and cost &#8377;41 is exactly that. Nothing on this site pretends otherwise.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2>Vote integrity</h2>
				<p>Votes on serials and chapters are deliberately hard to farm:</p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<strong>Human check.</strong> Voting passes through Cloudflare Turnstile,
						so bots can't stuff the ballot.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Dedup.</strong> One vote per subject per verified visitor.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>No shadow ranking.</strong> Vote counts shown are the counts
						recorded. Nothing is boosted or suppressed by the site.
					</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2>What this site is not</h2>
				<p>
					It is not a fully autonomous content farm. A human decides what serializes,
					what ships, and when a chapter isn't good enough to publish. It is also not a
					stunt: the stories are meant to be genuinely worth your reading time, and if
					they aren't, the provenance at least tells you exactly who to blame.
				</p>
				<p>
					Questions about how this all started? <a href="/about">The founder story is
					here.</a>
				</p>
			</section>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	.page {
		max-width: 720px;
		margin: 0 auto;
		padding: 2em 1em 4em;
		color: rgb(var(--gray-dark));
		line-height: 1.6;
	}
	.lede {
		font-size: 1.15em;
		color: rgb(var(--gray-dark));
	}
	section {
		margin-bottom: 1.5em;
	}
	h2 {
		margin: 1.25em 0 0.5em;
	}
	ul {
		padding-left: 1.25em;
		margin-bottom: 1em;
	}
	li {
		margin-bottom: 0.5em;
	}
	a {
		color: var(--accent);
		text-decoration: none;
	}
	a:hover {
		text-decoration: underline;
	}
</style>
